      "id": 2,
      "text": "Since 1929, the Holy See has resided in Vatican City, established as an independent state to enable the pope to exercise universal authority.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Vatican-City",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Papal influence in central Italy grew in the 5th century as the Roman Empire fell, with people relying on the pope for protection from invading armies.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        5
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Vatican-City",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Holy See’s authority extends over Catholics worldwide, making Vatican City the spiritual center for the global Catholic community.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Vatican-City",
      "category": "other",
      "quality": "poor",
      "qualityReason": "editorial_filler"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The Swiss Guard, formed in 1506, remains the personal security force for the pope.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.britannica.com/place/Vatican-City",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Vatican City’s astronomical observatory is one of the few in a sovereign microstate.",
